<title>The idea of the chain rule</title>

<p><ref>Video</ref> | <ref>Summary</ref> | <ref>Transcript</ref>  </p>

<description>
An illustration of the basic concept of the chain rule using interactive graphics to diagram the relevant points on the graphs and the corresponding slopes.
</description>

<section><title>Video</title>
<p>The chain rule gives us a way to calculate the derivative of a 
<ref to="doenet:function_composition">composition</ref>
 of functions, such as the composition <m>f(g(x))</m> of the functions <m>f</m> and <m>g</m>.  The chain rule can be tricky to apply correctly, especially since, with a complicated expression, one might need to use the chain rule multiple times.  Nonetheless, the idea of the chain rule can be understood fairly simply.</p>

<p>The following video outlines the basic idea of the chain rule.  In the remainder of this page, we illustrate the idea of the chain rule in three ways.  <ref to="doenet:function_machines">First</ref>, we illustrate the concept using 
<ref to="doenet:function_machine">function machines</ref>
.  <ref to="doenet:linear_functions">Second</ref>, we show how for linear functions, the chain rule is just the product of the slopes of the function graphs.  <ref to="doenet:nonlinear_functions">Third</ref>, we show that for nonlinear functions, the chain rule is just the product of the slopes of the tangent lines to the function graphs.  Examples of the chain rule are given on 
<ref to="doenet:chain_rule_simple_examples">another page</ref>
.</p>


