<aside name="chainRuleFormula"><title>Formula - The chain rule:</title>
  <md>
  <mrow>h'(x) \amp= f'(g(x)) \cdot g'(x)\;\;\;\text{where}\;\;\;h(x) = f(g(x))</mrow>
  </md>
  <md>
    <mrow>\textbf{Differential form:}\;\;\;\frac{dh}{dx} \amp= \frac{df}{dy} \cdot \frac{dy}{dx}\;\;\;\text{where}\;\;\;h = f(y)\;\;\text{and}\;\;y=g(x)</mrow>
  </md>
</aside>


  

<problem><title>Video review questions</title>

<setup>
  <function name="f" symbolic="true">7x</function>
  <function name="g" variables="y" symbolic="true">3y+4</function>
  <function name="h" variables="z" symbolic="true">z^3</function>
</setup>


<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title>Quick <ref to="https://mathinsight.org/function_composition">composition of functions</ref> review</title>
  <p>a) If <m>f(x) = 7x</m> and <m>g(y) = $g</m>, then <m>f(g(y)) = </m>
    <answer>$$f($$g(y))</answer>
  </p>
  <p>b) If <m>f(x) = $f</m> and <m>g(y) = $g</m>, then <m>\bigg(g \circ f\bigg)(x) = </m>
    <answer>$$g($$f(x))</answer>
  </p>
  <p>c) If <m>h(z) = $h</m> and <m>f(x) = $f</m>, then <m>\bigg(f \circ h\bigg)(z) = </m>
    <answer>$$f($$h(z))</answer>
  </p>
  <p>d) If <m>h(z) = $h</m> and <m>g(y) = $g</m>, then <m>h(g(y)) = </m>
    <answer>$$h($$g(y))</answer>
  </p>
   <hint><p>You actually don't have to expand out the binomial for the last part; part (d) will be marked correct as long as you have the right expression, (i.e. it could be of the form <m>(a + b)^c</m>).</p>
      <p>However, if you want to practice your algebra, it's easiest to use the <ref to="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binomial_theorem"><term>binomial theorem</term></ref> here to expand this expression.</p></hint>
</exercise>

<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title>Apply chain rule step-by-step</title>
<p>Let's solve for the derivative of the composition of two functions using the <term>chain rule</term>:</p>
<p>Let  <m> h(z) = $h</m>, and <m>g(y) = $g</m>. Compute the derivative of <m>h(g(y))</m>.</p>

<ol>
    <li><p>First, we need to calculate the 
      <answer name="ans1b" inline="true">
    <choice>approximation</choice>
    <choice credit="1">composition</choice>
    <choice>product</choice>
  </answer> of <m>h(z)</m> and <m>g(y)</m>.</p>
    <p>Great! If you wanted, at this point you could use the <ref to="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binomial_theorem"><term>binomial theorem</term></ref> and take the derivative of each term in your answer. But, using the <ref to="$chainRuleFormula">chain rule</ref> there's a much easier way. First, the unsimplified (or unexpanded) version of the composition <m>h(g(y))</m> is:</p>
      
      <p><m>h(g(y))</m> = $$h($$g(y))</p>
    </li>

   <li><p>Next, using the function symbols and variables specific to this problem,  we need to apply the <ref to="$chainRuleFormula">chain rule</ref>. Using the "prime" notation for the derivative, the general formula of the chain rule for this problem is given by: </p>
      <p>
      <m>h(g(y))' = </m> <mathInput name="mi1" /><m> '( g(y)) \cdot </m> <mathInput name="mi2" /></p>
        <answer name="ans1c" matchPartial="true">
          <award>
            <when>$mi1 = h and $mi2 = g'(y)</when>
          </award> 
        </answer>
     <feedback condition="$ans1c.numSubmissions > 0 and $ans1c.creditAchieved != 1"><p>Here, we are just trying to frame the general formula for the <ref to="$chainRuleFormula">chain rule</ref> in terms of the function symbols and variables of the problem.</p>
       <p>If you do this, you will get 
         <me>h'(y) = h'(g(y))\cdot g'(y)</me>
       </p>
     </feedback>
    </li>

  <li><p>Great job! One step to go. The first part of this formula is given by:</p>
    <m>h'(g(y)) = <math name="part1">3 $$g(y)^(3-1)</math></m>,
    <p>which you can simplify to:</p>
    <m>h'(g(y)) = </m> <answer name="ans1d"><math simplify="true" extend="$part1" /></answer>
    <feedback condition="$ans1d.numSubmissions > 0 and $ans1d.creditAchieved != 1">No need to multiply this all out, you can just subtract the exponents, giving <m>3(3y+4)^2</m></feedback>
    <p>Then we need to find the derivative of <m>g</m> with respect to <m>y</m>, which is:</p>
    <m>g'(y) = </m> <answer name="ans1e"><derivative variables="y">$g</derivative></answer>
    <feedback condition="$ans1e.numSubmissions > 0 and $ans1e.creditAchieved != 1"><p>Using the fact that the derivative of a linear function is a constant value, what is the derivative of <m>g(y) = 3y+4</m>?</p>
      <p><m>g'(y) = 3</m></p>
    </feedback>
    <p>Putting it all together, we have:</p>
    <p hide="$ans1e.creditAchieved != 1 or $ans1d.creditAchieved != 1">
    <m>h'(y) = </m> <math name="final">($ans1d) ($ans1e) </math></p>
    <p>which simplifies to:
    <m>h'(y) = </m><math simplify="true" extend="$final" />.</p>
  </li>
</ol>

</exercise>

</problem>


</section>

